\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If a Finsler metric on $S^2$ has one hyperbolic prime closed geodesic, must it have infinitely many geometrically distinct prime closed geodesics? Prime means that the geodesic is not an iterate; hyperbolic means that the linearized return map has no eigenvalue on the unit circle.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/geodesicsproblems.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
If a Finsler metric on $S^2$ has one hyperbolic prime closed geodesic, must it have infinitely many geometrically distinct prime closed geodesics? Prime means that the geodesic is not an iterate; hyperbolic means that the linearized return map has no eigenvalue on the unit circle.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20001871 (AIM-GEOMETRY-0209). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/geodesicsproblems.pdf}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:20001871}

