\section*{A hyperbolic geodesic and the robust infinite branch}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Infinitely many prime closed geodesics}: Every smooth Riemannian sphere of dimension at least two is claimed to have infinitely many prime closed geodesics with distinct images. The paper also obtains infinitude on every nonempty closed three-manifold.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Infinitely many prime closed geodesics}: Assuming finitely many prime images gives a uniform budget for local loop-space crossings, including degenerate root types. Reflection-equivariant cohomology and half-period translation compare ordinary and root-degree filtrations. Extra classes would force a quadratic discrepancy, but the exact-sequence and filling estimates allow only a linear budget; covering and three-manifold results provide the final transfer.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Infinitely many prime closed geodesics}: The theorem provides infinitely many geodesic images for every Riemannian sphere, so covers the Riemannian S2 subcase. Long source question permits general Finsler metrics, including irreversible ones; that extra class is not treated.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
\textit{Infinitely many prime closed geodesics}: The pinned catalogue lists no Lean formalization for this result.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item OpenAI, \emph{Infinitely many closed geodesic images on every Riemannian sphere}, 2026-09-24; citation key \texttt{OAI:Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026}. \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/paper.pdf}{PDF (GitHub)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/raw/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/paper.pdf}{PDF (direct)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/README.md}{Manuscript page and citation}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/paper.pdf}{Latest version}.
